  "query_preview": "High water for back Bays across Southern Delaware are a \nconcern too, so a Coastal Flood Warnings for Sussex County Delaware \nis in effect until Monday 2 PM.  \n\nFor Delaware Bay communities, a strengthening Northwest wind will \nremain across Delaware Bay into tonight. This will promote lowering \ntidal departures as the waters is pushed down/out Delaware Bay. As a \nresult, additional flooding (at high tides) will be spotty Minor or \nless. No additional flags for tidal flooding are expected. \n\nFor the tidal Delaware River, the North to Northeast flow has kept \nwater levels (at high tide) at the lower part of Minor flooding \nrange. No flags for tidal flooding are expected. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dangerous rip currents and surf threats have\ndiminished.\n\nThe Nor'easter came onshore across NJ earlier today. Behind it\nthe winds and seas have continued to diminish. The seas have\ndropped to mostly 5 to 6 ft at the buoys as of 5PM. These\nconditions indicate that High Surf is no longer a threat to the\nshore areas. While Rip Currents are still present, the strength \nof these has diminished somewhat too. We are calling for a \nMODERATE risk for Rip Currents on Monday. Continue to exercise\ncaution if you will be near the ocean tonight and Monday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...A cold front is likely to cross through the region \nFriday into Saturday.\n\nThe surface high that will be over the region for much of the week \nwill start to build southeast and off shore by Thursday. This will \nopen the door for a cold front to sweep through the region Friday or \nSaturday. Models for the most part, aren't depicting a widespread \nrain event with this front. However, southwesterly flow on Wednesday \nand Thursday, should set the stage with ample moisture in place. The \nbiggest question is forcing with this front. The best synoptic scale \nlift will likely be well removed from the mesoscale forcing \nassociated with the front. For now the 20 to 30 percent PoPs through \nthis period seem appropriate this far out. Preliminary guidance \nindicates that temperatures behind the front could be 5 to 10 \ndegrees colder than normal.",
